1
00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:03.269
In this course, you'll build an AI agent
from scratch and learn how

2
00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.471
to evaluate it.

3
00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:07.474
In this lesson, we'll dive
into the details of this agent structure

4
00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:10.677
and then go through an agent example
that can perform data analysis.

5
00:00:11.211 --> 00:00:14.214
You'll then learn how to code
such an agent in Python.

6
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.049
All right. Let's go.

7
00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:19.352
Agents are typically made up
of three main types of components:

8
00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:22.689
a router, skills,
and then memory and state.

9
00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.493
The image on the right here
shows an example of an agent

10
00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:28.361
with a router in black,

11
00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:30.463
the state
as the dark purple at the bottom,

12
00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:33.633
and then a set of three different skills
that that agent can call in this case

13
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.636
track package,
look up item, and answer a question.

14
00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:39.639
Each of those different components
can interact with the state,

15
00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:40.140
and the router

16
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.843
is responsible for handling user input
and then output back to the user.

17
00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:47.781
Looking first at the router, the router is
the main planner of the agent.

18
00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:50.216
Sort of the agent's brain in a way.

19
00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:53.286
The router is responsible for deciding
which skill or function

20
00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:55.822
the agent will call to answer
a user's question.

21
00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:58.958
So when the router receives user
input or a response back

22
00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:02.662
from a different skill, the router will
then decide which other skill to call

23
00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:03.763
from there.

24
00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:05.565
Routers can take a few different forms.

25
00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:07.067
They can either be an LLM

26
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:10.070
equipped with function calling,
as we'll use in this course,

27
00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:14.340
or a more simple NLP classifier,
or even just a rules-based code.

28
00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:18.078
Generally,
the simpler type of router that you have,

29
00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:19.846
the better your performance will be in,

30
00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:21.848
the more consistent
your performance will be,

31
00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:24.551
but you'll also scope down
the capabilities of that router.

32
00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:27.687
Something like an LLM with function
calling has a very broad ranging

33
00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:31.391
capabilities, but is a little bit
more unreliable than rules-based code.

34
00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:34.694
That's also where evaluation can help
you close the gap.

35
00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:37.263
Some agents,
instead of using a single router

36
00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:40.266
step, will distribute that logic
throughout the agent.

37
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.537
Popular frameworks that use this are line
graph as well as OpenAI swarm.

38
00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:47.974
They still have routing logic,
but instead of having one single router

39
00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:52.579
step, they distribute that responsibility
throughout the agent itself.

40
00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.849
Next, skills are the

41
00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:59.352
individual logic blocks and capabilities
that an agent has.

42
00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:02.355
So, skills are what allow the agent
to actually do anything that would

43
00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:07.160
allow it to connect with the outside world
through APIs or call databases,

44
00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:10.563
or really do any of the different tasks
that your agent can accomplish.

45
00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:14.167
Every agent will have either one
or more skills.

46
00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:17.070
An agent without any skills
doesn't really do anything.

47
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.272
Not a real use case there.

48
00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:22.375
Skills are made up of individual steps.
LLM calls,

49
00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:26.312
application code, API calls, or really
any other code that you want to use

50
00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:27.647
there.

51
00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:30.650
One example of a skill
that's very common is a RAG skill.

52
00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:31.351
So in this case,

53
00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:34.888
you could have a retrieval augmented
generation capability within your agent

54
00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:38.258
that would handle everything
from embeddings to looking up data

55
00:02:38.258 --> 00:02:42.228
from a vector database to making an LLM
call with retrieved context.

56
00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:45.298
And all of that is encompassed
in a single RAG skill.

57
00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:48.301
So you can see how skills can encompass
multiple different steps,

58
00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:51.404
and entire LLM applications can be thought of

59
00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:54.407
as skills in the context of agents.

60
00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:57.443
Once skills is complete,
they'll also return back to the router

61
00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.012
in most agent contexts

62
00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:02.048
so that they can choose either
to return to the user or call

63
00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:02.882
another skill from there.

64
00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.085
Memory and state are also used by agents

65
00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:09.722
to store information that can be accessed
by every component within the agent.

66
00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:13.226
Typically, memory and state are used
to store things like retrieved-context,

67
00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:18.231
configuration variables, or very commonly,
a log of previous agent execution steps.

68
00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:21.568
This last one is probably the most common
that you'll see.

69
00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:24.604
Many LLM APIs actually rely on being passed

70
00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:27.607
in a whole dictionary or list of messages

71
00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:31.177
about what the agent has done previously,
before choosing the next step to call.

72
00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:35.215
The OpenAI function calling router
that you'll use throughout the course

73
00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:38.518
uses this approach, so you'll get
very familiar with that approach.

74
00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:42.622
Turning to your example agent that you're
going to build throughout this course.

75
00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:46.526
The example agent that you're going to
create is a data analysis assistant

76
00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:50.463
that can help you understand
and ask questions over a sales database

77
00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:51.497
that you have.

78
00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:53.466
That agent has a few different skills.

79
00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:57.170
It has a data lookup skill that can query
information from that attached database.

80
00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:01.040
It has a data analysis skill
that can draw conclusions from that data,

81
00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:04.244
spot trends and make calculations.

82
00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:07.013
And then finally,
it has a data visualization skill

83
00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:11.484
that can generate Python code
and create graphs through that code

84
00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:12.285
and visualizations.

85
00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:15.688
Looking
at your example agent visually here

86
00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:19.058
you see you have a user who's
sending in queries to a router

87
00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:22.462
in this case
GPT-4o-mini call with function calling.

88
00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:25.865
And then that router will call
one of three different tools here,

89
00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:29.702
the lookup sales data tool, data
analysis tool or data visualization tool.

90
00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:33.239
And then those tools will return
back to the router, which then decides

91
00:04:33.239 --> 00:04:36.242
to either return to the user
or call another tool from there.

92
00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:40.079
Now we're using the word tool here
because that's what's expected by

93
00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:41.247
GPT-4o-mini.

94
00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:43.149
That's analogous to skills in this case.

95
00:04:43.149 --> 00:04:46.819
So your lookup sales data tool would be
analogous to a lookup sales data skill.

96
00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:50.490
Diving into each of these skills
a little bit more deeply,

97
00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.625
you see that each
skill has a few different steps

98
00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:55.628
that it can actually go through
to accomplish its task.

99
00:04:55.695 --> 00:04:59.565
The Lookup sales data tool, for example,
first prepares a database

100
00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:01.534
so it loads in a local database
and make sure

101
00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:04.037
that it's ready to query that database.

102
00:05:04.037 --> 00:05:08.474
Then it will generate SQL using another
LLM call to query that database,

103
00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:12.211
and then finally execute that SQL
and return the result

104
00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:13.246
all the way back to the router.

105
00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.315
Similarly, the data

106
00:05:15.315 --> 00:05:18.618
analysis tool makes a single call
to generate an analysis.

107
00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:21.821
So it just makes a single LLM call
and then returns that response

108
00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:23.189
back to the router.

109
00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:23.690
And finally,

110
00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.093
the data visualization tool actually
makes two sequential LLM calls.

111
00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:29.662
First, to generate a chart config

112
00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:32.732
and then to generate Python code
based on that config.

113
00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:38.237
The reason for the two calls in that case
is that while you could just ask an LLM

114
00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:41.240
to generate code straight away
and do both steps at once,

115
00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:44.310
you'll get more unreliable responses
that way.

116
00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.980
And because chart visualizations in Python
are somewhat formulaic,

117
00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:52.185
it helps to first generate this chart
config with a few key variables

118
00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:55.722
and then generate the Python code
based on that chart config.

119
00:05:55.922 --> 00:05:58.758
So splitting up the task into two simpler
tasks instead of

120
00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:01.994
asking for the LLM
to complete one more difficult task.

121
00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:04.130
In this lesson,

122
00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:08.201
you've learned about the major components
of agents, routers, skills, and memory,

123
00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:11.637
and you've examined the example agent
that you're going to build.

124
00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:12.772
In the next video,

125
00:06:12.772 --> 00:06:15.575
you're going to go through a notebook
and actually implement and build

126
00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:16.409
this example agent.

